Usage: xmd agent [OPTIONS] [action] [agent]

Arguments:
   [action]                  the one action: `options`
   [agent]                   agent to ask (default: the agent `xmd run` would use)

Options:
   --model [MODEL]           read effort choices for this exact model id instead of the current one
   --json                    write the choices as version-1 JSON instead of text [default: false]
   -h, --help                show help

PLUGINS
  --plugin <specifier>  load a Plugin before the command runs (repeatable)

A Plugin is a package or a file this invocation names. They install in the
order they are written, and that order is the whole order: xmd ships no
Plugin and installs none unless one is named.

A selected Plugin is trusted executable code, not a sandboxed extension:
loading one runs its module, and selected Plugin code can execute whatever
the surrounding runtime permits. xmd discovers none on its own — a package
that happens to be installed does nothing until it is named — and loads none
over the network.

Inspecting an agent reads the choices it advertises for a session, so xmd
creates one, reads it, and closes it without sending a prompt. The provider
may keep that empty conversation in its own history.

The command runs no document, spends no model turn, writes no xmd journal and
creates no durable xmd session.

  xmd agent options
  xmd agent options codex
  xmd agent options codex --model gpt-5.4
  xmd agent options codex --model gpt-5.4 --json

Ids are exact provider ids, and they are what a <Session> writes:
  <Session name="architect" model="gpt-5.4" effort="high">
