   -V, --verbose             log journal entries to stderr [default: false]
   --raw                     output raw markdown without normalization or terminal formatting [default: false]
   --secret-detection        scan durable events for credentials before they persist; disable with --no-secret-detection [default: true]
   --json                    write the inspection result as JSON (status, list and history only) [default: false]
   --forkable                add each event's forkability to the history (history only) [default: false]
   --status [STATUS]         list only runs retaining this status
   --artifact [ARTIFACT]     inspect this sealed .xmd artifact instead of a retained run (status and history only)
   --output [OUTPUT]         the .xmd file to write the artifact to (export only); the artifact contains this run's complete retained Workspace, which may include source, generated files and secrets — treat it as confidential
   -h, --help                show help

PLUGINS
  --plugin <specifier>  load a Plugin before the command runs (repeatable)

A Plugin is a package or a file this invocation names. They install in the
order they are written, and that order is the whole order: xmd ships no
Plugin and installs none unless one is named.

A selected Plugin is trusted executable code, not a sandboxed extension:
loading one runs its module, and selected Plugin code can execute whatever
the surrounding runtime permits. xmd discovers none on its own — a package
that happens to be installed does nothing until it is named — and loads none
over the network.

